WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.280
"Is Soil Degradation Reducing
the Nutrition of Fruits and Vegetables?"

00:00:06.280 --> 00:00:08.120
Internet articles
and the popular press

00:00:08.120 --> 00:00:09.080
frequently state that

00:00:09.080 --> 00:00:11.600
the mineral nutrient
composition of crops

00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:14.040
has been declining
over the last 50 years

00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:16.920
with titles like
"The great nutrient collapse,"

00:00:16.920 --> 00:00:20.960
blamed in part on the purported
nutrient depletion of the soil.

00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:23.000
This narrative is touted
by the leading

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:25.960
dietary supplement
trade association,

00:00:25.960 --> 00:00:27.760
but is it true?

00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:29.560
This is probably
the most cited study,

00:00:29.560 --> 00:00:32.080
comparing the nutrient content
of U.S. fruits and vegetables

00:00:32.080 --> 00:00:34.640
between 1950 and 1999,

00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:35.800
and about half,

00:00:35.800 --> 00:00:37.960
seven of the 13
nutrients tested,

00:00:37.960 --> 00:00:40.360
showed no significant decline,

00:00:40.360 --> 00:00:41.680
and the six that did

00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:45.000
only declined by about
15 percent on average.

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:47.800
A similar study in the UK
looking at mineral content

00:00:47.800 --> 00:00:50.600
had similar findings
with declines in about half.

00:00:50.600 --> 00:00:52.400
Of course, the drop in sodium
doesn't matter;

00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:54.080
we get too much of it as it is,

00:00:54.080 --> 00:00:56.440
and most people
get more than enough copper.

00:00:56.440 --> 00:00:59.680
But the ten percent drop
in magnesium is significant,

00:00:59.680 --> 00:01:02.520
and though the drop in iron
looks precipitous,

00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:04.040
the absolute drop is tiny,

00:01:04.040 --> 00:01:07.280
since fruits and veggies
have so little to begin with.

00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:09.640
Australian researchers
had similar findings:

00:01:09.640 --> 00:01:12.520
a halving of iron in fruit
over the last few decades,

00:01:12.520 --> 00:01:15.560
but the absolute drop
is like 0.2 mg per serving,

00:01:15.560 --> 00:01:17.040
which is about 1/50th

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:19.440
of the recommended
daily allowance for most people,

00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:20.920
and the same with zinc.

00:01:20.920 --> 00:01:23.560
And there was actually
an increase in grains.

00:01:23.560 --> 00:01:26.360
As you can see, there was just
a few milligrams difference

00:01:26.360 --> 00:01:27.920
in calcium or magnesium,

00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:29.920
where the RDA is in
the hundreds of milligrams.

00:01:30.640 --> 00:01:33.320
So, with such small
absolute differences,

00:01:33.320 --> 00:01:35.320
any true decrease
in mineral content

00:01:35.320 --> 00:01:37.040
is unlikely
to have a large impact

00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:38.360
on nutritional intake.

00:01:38.360 --> 00:01:42.520
And it's not clear
if these changes are even real.

00:01:42.520 --> 00:01:45.120
These comparisons of historical
food composition tables

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:47.800
are not a reliable way
to determine changes

00:01:47.800 --> 00:01:50.240
in nutrient composition
of foods over time,

00:01:50.240 --> 00:01:53.440
since they are just
unmatched snapshots in time

00:01:53.440 --> 00:01:54.680
not meant to sync up.

00:01:54.680 --> 00:01:57.440
So, these may have been
different varieties,

00:01:57.440 --> 00:01:59.000
or there may have been
differences

00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:01.720
in ripeness, season, location.

00:02:01.720 --> 00:02:03.160
If you look at
the levels of minerals

00:02:03.160 --> 00:02:04.480
in the soil itself,

00:02:04.480 --> 00:02:07.000
you don't tend
to see declining levels,

00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:08.280
and in some cases,
you actually see

00:02:08.280 --> 00:02:10.200
a significant rise
in mineral levels

00:02:10.200 --> 00:02:13.160
nearly across the board
over the last half century.

00:02:13.160 --> 00:02:14.920
So, where is this gloomy

00:02:14.920 --> 00:02:18.240
supplement industry propaganda
coming from?

00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:20.440
There's been a concern
that rising CO2 levels

00:02:20.440 --> 00:02:22.480
might reduce nutrients in crops,

00:02:22.480 --> 00:02:24.120
but the largest recorded change

00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:27.320
was a 59 percent increase
in total antioxidant content,

00:02:27.320 --> 00:02:30.160
thanks to higher
phytonutrient levels.

00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:32.240
What about the concern
that modern varieties

00:02:32.240 --> 00:02:34.320
grow so much larger and faster,

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:35.840
potentially creating a trade-off

00:02:35.840 --> 00:02:39.560
between yield
and nutrient concentration?

00:02:39.560 --> 00:02:41.400
One way to study this
is to grow a whole bunch

00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:43.400
of different varieties
released over the years

00:02:43.400 --> 00:02:45.400
under the same conditions,

00:02:45.400 --> 00:02:47.680
which is what these researchers
did with broccoli.

00:02:47.680 --> 00:02:50.800
14 cultivars
released over 50 years,

00:02:50.800 --> 00:02:53.080
and they found that there was
no clear change over time

00:02:53.080 --> 00:02:54.280
in mineral content,

00:02:54.280 --> 00:02:56.760
though they did find
that larger heads of broccoli

00:02:56.760 --> 00:02:58.640
did tend to have
lower mineral content;

00:02:58.640 --> 00:03:01.440
something to keep in mind
next time you shop.

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:04.680
As for the dietary supplement
trade association's claim

00:03:04.680 --> 00:03:07.880
that Americans don't get the
nutrients they need from foods?

00:03:07.880 --> 00:03:10.320
Well, for example,
only half of Americans

00:03:10.320 --> 00:03:13.640
reach even 75 mg
of vitamin C a day--

00:03:13.640 --> 00:03:16.600
less than the amount
in a single orange.

00:03:16.600 --> 00:03:18.160
So, why aren't Americans

00:03:18.160 --> 00:03:20.600
getting enough fruit
and vegetable nutrients?

00:03:20.600 --> 00:03:23.080
Because they aren't eating
enough fruits and vegetables.

00:03:23.080 --> 00:03:26.160
They don't need pills,
they just need produce.

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:27.600
So, even if there really is

00:03:27.600 --> 00:03:30.840
15 percent less vitamin C
in today's broccoli,

00:03:30.840 --> 00:03:32.480
that just may mean
you have to eat

00:03:32.480 --> 00:03:34.320
six florets of today's broccoli

00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:38.398
instead of five florets
from yesteryear.

